---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="linear-algebra"
  title="Change of Basis"
  description="An arrow is a thing in the plane; its coordinates are only a description of it, written in whichever grid you happen to use. Two people watch the same arrow through two different grids and write down two different pairs of numbers. The matrix of basis vectors is the dictionary between them, and the sandwich P⁻¹AP is what a transformation looks like once it is translated."
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-stage">
      <canvas id="cob" width="920" height="700"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <div class="control">
        <label>Their first basis vector b₁</label>
        <div class="mgrid">
          <div><label>x <b id="b1xval">2.00</b></label><input type="range" id="b1x" min="-3" max="3" step="0.05" value="2" /></div>
          <div><label>y <b id="b1yval">1.00</b></label><input type="range" id="b1y" min="-3" max="3" step="0.05" value="1" /></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Their second basis vector b₂</label>
        <div class="mgrid">
          <div><label>x <b id="b2xval">-1.00</b></label><input type="range" id="b2x" min="-3" max="3" step="0.05" value="-1" /></div>
          <div><label>y <b id="b2yval">1.00</b></label><input type="range" id="b2y" min="-3" max="3" step="0.05" value="1" /></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>The vector v, in our coordinates</label>
        <div class="mgrid">
          <div><label>x <b id="vxval">3.00</b></label><input type="range" id="vx" min="-5" max="5" step="0.05" value="3" /></div>
          <div><label>y <b id="vyval">1.00</b></label><input type="range" id="vy" min="-5" max="5" step="0.05" value="1" /></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Presets for the basis</label>
        <div class="seg" id="presets">
          <button data-b="2,1,-1,1" class="active">Skewed</button>
          <button data-b="1,0,0,1">Ours</button>
          <button data-b="0.71,0.71,-0.71,0.71">Rotated</button>
          <button data-b="2,0,0,0.5">Stretched</button>
          <button data-b="1,1,2,2">Degenerate</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="foreign" /> Show the other grid in each panel</label>

      <div class="control">
        <label>A transformation, described in their grid</label>
        <div class="seg" id="map">
          <button data-t="none" class="active">None</button>
          <button data-t="stretch">Stretch b₁ ×2</button>
          <button data-t="flip">Flip b₂</button>
          <button data-t="rot">Quarter turn</button>
          <button data-t="shear">Shear</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="readout">
        <div class="row"><span>v in our coordinates</span><b id="veOut">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span>v in their coordinates</span><b id="vbOut">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span>det P</span><b id="detOut">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span>A in our grid</span><b id="aeOut">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span>A′ in their grid</span><b id="abOut">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span>Av, our coordinates</span><b id="avOut">—</b></div>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-faint);margin:0;line-height:1.5">
        Drag b₁, b₂ or v in either panel. The two pictures always hold the same
        arrow; only the ruled paper underneath is different.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      Both panels show <b>the same arrow</b>. On the left it is measured against
      our square grid, on the right against theirs, which we have redrawn as if
      it were the square one, because that is how it looks from inside. The
      arrow never moved. Only the numbers written under it did.
    </li>
    <li>
      Drag <b>v</b> and watch the two coordinate readouts disagree. Then drag
      <b>b₁</b> instead: v stays exactly where it is on the left, yet its
      coordinates on the right change completely. Coordinates are a statement
      about the <b>pair</b> (vector, basis), never about the vector alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      The columns of <b>P</b> are just b₁ and b₂ written in our coordinates, so
      P takes their numbers and returns ours: <b>v = P[v]<sub>B</sub></b>. It
      reads in their language, and it is easy to remember which way round it
      goes because the columns are literally where their basis vectors are.
      Going the other way needs <b>P⁻¹</b>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pick the <b>Degenerate</b> preset. b₁ and b₂ line up, det P is zero, and
      the right-hand grid collapses. Two vectors that do not span the plane
      cannot describe every point of it, so there is no dictionary to invert:
      <b>a basis has to be independent</b> or nothing works.
    </li>
    <li>
      Switch on <b>Stretch b₁ ×2</b>. In their grid the map is as simple as a
      map gets: multiply the first coordinate by two and leave the second alone,
      a <b>diagonal</b> matrix. Look at what the very same map has to be written
      as in our grid and it is a mess of four unrelated numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      That mess is <b>A = P A′ P⁻¹</b>, and reading it right to left tells you
      exactly what it does: translate our numbers into theirs, do the easy thing
      there, translate back. <b>Diagonalising</b> a matrix is nothing more than
      hunting for the basis in which it was easy all along, and the eigenvectors
      are that basis.
    </li>
    <li>
      Rotate the basis with the <b>Rotated</b> preset and check det P. When the
      new basis is orthonormal, det P is 1, lengths and angles survive the
      translation, and P⁻¹ is simply Pᵀ. That is the case worth remembering, and
      the reason orthonormal bases are worth the trouble of building.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText } from '../../../lib/sim';
  import { equationBar } from '../../../lib/equation';

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Left panel: the plane drawn on our paper. Right panel: the same plane drawn
  // on theirs, which is what you get by pushing everything through P⁻¹ first,
  // since P⁻¹b₁ = ê₁. So both panels run the same drawing code with a different
  // matrix in front of it, which is exactly the claim the page is making.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  type Mat = { a: number; b: number; c: number; d: number };
  type Vec = { x: number; y: number };

  const I: Mat = { a: 1, b: 0, c: 0, d: 1 };
  const b1: Vec = { x: 2, y: 1 };
  const b2: Vec = { x: -1, y: 1 };
  const v: Vec = { x: 3, y: 1 };
  const state = { map: 'none', foreign: false };

  /** In their grid the maps are the simple ones. Ours has to work for it. */
  const MAPS: Record<string, { m: Mat; name: string }> = {
    none: { m: I, name: 'nothing' },
    stretch: { m: { a: 2, b: 0, c: 0, d: 1 }, name: 'stretch b₁ ×2' },
    flip: { m: { a: 1, b: 0, c: 0, d: -1 }, name: 'flip b₂' },
    rot: { m: { a: 0, b: -1, c: 1, d: 0 }, name: 'quarter turn in their grid' },
    shear: { m: { a: 1, b: 1, c: 0, d: 1 }, name: 'shear along b₁' },
  };

  const det = (m: Mat) => m.a * m.d - m.b * m.c;
  const mul = (p: Mat, q: Mat): Mat => ({
    a: p.a * q.a + p.b * q.c,
    b: p.a * q.b + p.b * q.d,
    c: p.c * q.a + p.d * q.c,
    d: p.c * q.b + p.d * q.d,
  });
  const app = (m: Mat, p: Vec): Vec => ({
    x: m.a * p.x + m.b * p.y,
    y: m.c * p.x + m.d * p.y,
  });
  function inv(m: Mat): Mat | null {
    const D = det(m);
    if (Math.abs(D) < 1e-6) return null;
    return { a: m.d / D, b: -m.b / D, c: -m.c / D, d: m.a / D };
  }
  const P = (): Mat => ({ a: b1.x, b: b2.x, c: b1.y, d: b2.y });

  // --- canvas ----------------------------------------------------------------

  const canvas = document.getElementById('cob') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => draw());
  const cssv = (n: string) => getComputedStyle(canvas).getPropertyValue(n).trim();
  const inkA = (a: number) => `rgba(${cssv('--stage-ink') || '232,233,236'},${a})`;
  const { label } = makeText(ctx, inkA);
  const setEq = equationBar();

  const ACC = '#7c8cff'; // b₁
  const TEAL = '#5eead4'; // b₂
  const WARM = '#f59e0b'; // v
  const HOT = '#f9736a'; // Av

  interface Panel {
    x: number;
    y: number;
    w: number;
    h: number;
    T: Mat; // what to push the world through before drawing it
    title: string;
    sub: string;
    native: 'ours' | 'theirs';
  }

  const UNIT = 46;
  const PANELS = (): Panel[] => {
    const Pi = inv(P());
    return [
      {
        x: 18, y: 34, w: 434, h: 434,
        T: I,
        title: 'OUR GRID',
        sub: 'the standard basis, drawn square',
        native: 'ours',
      },
      {
        x: 468, y: 34, w: 434, h: 434,
        T: Pi ?? I,
        title: 'THEIR GRID',
        sub: Pi ? 'b₁ and b₂ drawn square, which is how they see it' : 'no grid: b₁ and b₂ do not span the plane',
        native: 'theirs',
      },
    ];
  };
  const centre = (p: Panel) => ({ x: p.x + p.w / 2, y: p.y + p.h / 2 });

  function screen(p: Panel, w: Vec) {
    const t = app(p.T, w);
    const c = centre(p);
    return { px: c.x + t.x * UNIT, py: c.y - t.y * UNIT };
  }

  // --- drawing primitives ----------------------------------------------------

  function frame(p: Panel) {
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.1);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(p.x, p.y, p.w, p.h);
    label(p.title, p.x + 2, p.y - 16, 0.6, 'left', 11);
    label(p.sub, p.x + 2, p.y - 2, 0.35, 'left', 10);
  }

  /** Both lattices in one panel: the native one solid, the foreign one dotted. */
  function lattices(p: Panel) {
    const R = 8;
    const draw2 = (u: Vec, w: Vec, col: string, dash: number[], alpha: number, lw: number) => {
      if (Math.abs(u.x * w.y - u.y * w.x) < 1e-4) return;
      ctx.strokeStyle = col;
      ctx.globalAlpha = alpha;
      ctx.lineWidth = lw;
      ctx.setLineDash(dash);
      ctx.beginPath();
      for (let k = -R; k <= R; k++) {
        const a = screen(p, { x: u.x * k - w.x * R, y: u.y * k - w.y * R });
        const b = screen(p, { x: u.x * k + w.x * R, y: u.y * k + w.y * R });
        ctx.moveTo(a.px, a.py);
        ctx.lineTo(b.px, b.py);
        const c = screen(p, { x: w.x * k - u.x * R, y: w.y * k - u.y * R });
        const d = screen(p, { x: w.x * k + u.x * R, y: w.y * k + u.y * R });
        ctx.moveTo(c.px, c.py);
        ctx.lineTo(d.px, d.py);
      }
      ctx.stroke();
      ctx.setLineDash([]);
      ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    };
    const ours = p.native === 'ours';
    // the grid this panel is not written in, only when asked for
    if (state.foreign)
      draw2(
        ours ? b1 : { x: 1, y: 0 },
        ours ? b2 : { x: 0, y: 1 },
        ours ? ACC : inkA(1),
        [3, 4],
        ours ? 0.3 : 0.22,
        1
      );
    draw2(
      ours ? { x: 1, y: 0 } : b1,
      ours ? { x: 0, y: 1 } : b2,
      cssv('--border') || '#262a32',
      [],
      0.95,
      1
    );
  }

  function arrow(p: Panel, w: Vec, col: string, text: string, lw = 3) {
    const c = centre(p);
    const t = screen(p, w);
    ctx.strokeStyle = col;
    ctx.fillStyle = col;
    ctx.lineWidth = lw;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(c.x, c.y);
    ctx.lineTo(t.px, t.py);
    ctx.stroke();
    const ang = Math.atan2(t.py - c.y, t.px - c.x);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(t.px, t.py);
    ctx.lineTo(t.px - 12 * Math.cos(ang - 0.4), t.py - 12 * Math.sin(ang - 0.4));
    ctx.lineTo(t.px - 12 * Math.cos(ang + 0.4), t.py - 12 * Math.sin(ang + 0.4));
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
    if (text) label(text, t.px + 8, t.py - 8, 1, 'left', 12, col);
    return t;
  }

  /** v = p·b₁ + q·b₂, drawn as the two legs you actually walk. */
  function decomposition(p: Panel, coords: Vec) {
    const c = centre(p);
    const leg = screen(p, { x: b1.x * coords.x, y: b1.y * coords.x });
    const end = screen(p, v);
    ctx.setLineDash([5, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
    ctx.strokeStyle = ACC;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(c.x, c.y);
    ctx.lineTo(leg.px, leg.py);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.strokeStyle = TEAL;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(leg.px, leg.py);
    ctx.lineTo(end.px, end.py);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    label(`${coords.x.toFixed(2)} b_1`, (c.x + leg.px) / 2, (c.y + leg.py) / 2 - 7, 0.85, 'center', 11, ACC);
    label(`${coords.y.toFixed(2)} b_2`, (leg.px + end.px) / 2 + 6, (leg.py + end.py) / 2, 0.85, 'left', 11, TEAL);
  }

  // --- matrices printed on the canvas ---------------------------------------

  function matrix(x: number, y: number, m: Mat, col: string, name: string) {
    const cells = [
      [m.a.toFixed(2), m.b.toFixed(2)],
      [m.c.toFixed(2), m.d.toFixed(2)],
    ];
    const colW = 46, rowH = 19;
    const w = colW * 2 + 12, h = rowH * 2 + 10;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.35);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x + 6, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + 6, y + h);
    ctx.moveTo(x + w - 6, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w - 6, y + h);
    ctx.stroke();
    for (let r = 0; r < 2; r++)
      for (let c = 0; c < 2; c++)
        label(cells[r][c], x + 10 + c * colW + colW - 12, y + 18 + r * rowH, 0.9, 'right', 11.5, col);
    label(name, x + w / 2, y - 8, 0.85, 'center', 12, col);
    return w;
  }

  function vecBox(x: number, y: number, p: Vec, col: string, name: string) {
    const h = 48, w = 62;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.35);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x + 6, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + 6, y + h);
    ctx.moveTo(x + w - 6, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w - 6, y + h);
    ctx.stroke();
    label(p.x.toFixed(2), x + w - 10, y + 19, 0.95, 'right', 11.5, col);
    label(p.y.toFixed(2), x + w - 10, y + 38, 0.95, 'right', 11.5, col);
    label(name, x + w / 2, y - 8, 0.8, 'center', 11, col);
    return w;
  }

  function connector(x1: number, y1: number, x2: number, y2: number, text: string, col: string) {
    ctx.strokeStyle = col;
    ctx.fillStyle = col;
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x1, y1);
    ctx.lineTo(x2, y2);
    ctx.stroke();
    const ang = Math.atan2(y2 - y1, x2 - x1);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x2, y2);
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - 8 * Math.cos(ang - 0.4), y2 - 8 * Math.sin(ang - 0.4));
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - 8 * Math.cos(ang + 0.4), y2 - 8 * Math.sin(ang + 0.4));
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
    const mx = (x1 + x2) / 2, my = (y1 + y2) / 2;
    const vertical = Math.abs(y2 - y1) > Math.abs(x2 - x1);
    label(text, vertical ? mx + 9 : mx, vertical ? my + 4 : my - 8, 0.9, vertical ? 'left' : 'center', 11.5, col);
  }

  /**
   * The square that says it all: go across in our language, or go down, across
   * in theirs, and back up. Same destination, which is the definition of what
   * P⁻¹AP means.
   */
  function diagram(Pm: Mat, Pi: Mat | null, Ab: Mat, Ae: Mat, vb: Vec, av: Vec, avb: Vec) {
    const y0 = 516;
    label('THE SAME MAP, WRITTEN TWICE', 18, y0 - 18, 0.6, 'left', 11);

    const rowTop = y0 + 26;
    const rowBot = y0 + 122;
    const xL = 90, xR = 350;

    vecBox(xL, rowTop, vb, TEAL, '[v]_B');
    vecBox(xR, rowTop, avb, TEAL, '[Av]_B');
    vecBox(xL, rowBot, v, WARM, 'v');
    vecBox(xR, rowBot, av, HOT, 'Av');

    connector(xL + 74, rowTop + 24, xR - 12, rowTop + 24, "A′", TEAL);
    connector(xL + 74, rowBot + 24, xR - 12, rowBot + 24, 'A', HOT);
    connector(xL + 31, rowTop + 60, xL + 31, rowBot - 14, 'P', ACC);
    connector(xR + 31, rowBot - 14, xR + 31, rowTop + 60, 'P⁻¹', ACC);

    // and the matrices themselves: the dictionary, and the map in each language
    const mx = 560;
    matrix(mx, rowTop, Pm, ACC, 'P = [b₁ b₂]');
    if (!Pi) label('P⁻¹ does not exist', mx + 170, rowTop + 28, 0.9, 'left', 12, HOT);
    matrix(mx, rowBot, Ab, TEAL, 'A′, their grid');
    matrix(mx + 170, rowBot, Ae, HOT, 'A = P A′ P⁻¹');
  }

  // --- the frame -------------------------------------------------------------

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    const Pm = P();
    const Pi = inv(Pm);
    const Ab = MAPS[state.map].m;
    const Ae = Pi ? mul(mul(Pm, Ab), Pi) : I;
    const vb = Pi ? app(Pi, v) : { x: NaN, y: NaN };
    const av = app(Ae, v);
    const avb = app(Ab, vb);

    for (const p of PANELS()) {
      frame(p);
      ctx.save();
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.rect(p.x + 1, p.y + 1, p.w - 2, p.h - 2);
      ctx.clip();

      if (p.native === 'theirs' && !Pi) {
        label('b₁ and b₂ are parallel,', p.x + p.w / 2, p.y + p.h / 2 - 10, 0.6, 'center', 13, HOT);
        label('so there is no grid to draw', p.x + p.w / 2, p.y + p.h / 2 + 12, 0.4, 'center', 12);
        ctx.restore();
        continue;
      }

      lattices(p);

      // their basis, drawn where it lands in this panel; ours IS the square
      // grid, so it needs no arrows of its own
      arrow(p, b1, ACC, 'b₁');
      arrow(p, b2, TEAL, 'b₂');

      if (Number.isFinite(vb.x)) decomposition(p, vb);
      arrow(p, v, WARM, 'v');
      if (state.map !== 'none') {
        arrow(p, av, HOT, 'Av');
      }

      // what this panel calls the vector
      const shownCoords = p.native === 'ours' ? v : vb;
      label(
        `v reads (${shownCoords.x.toFixed(2)}, ${shownCoords.y.toFixed(2)}) here`,
        p.x + 10,
        p.y + p.h - 12,
        0.7,
        'left',
        12,
        WARM
      );
      ctx.restore();
    }

    // a one-line reminder of which way the dictionary runs
    const midX = 460;
    label('same arrow', midX, 26, 0.5, 'center', 11);
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.12);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 5]);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(midX, 40);
    ctx.lineTo(midX, 470);
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);

    diagram(Pm, Pi, Ab, Ae, vb, av, avb);

    // --- readouts
    const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
    const f = (p: Vec) => `(${p.x.toFixed(2)}, ${p.y.toFixed(2)})`;
    const fm = (m: Mat) => `[${m.a.toFixed(2)} ${m.b.toFixed(2)}; ${m.c.toFixed(2)} ${m.d.toFixed(2)}]`;
    el('veOut').textContent = f(v);
    el('vbOut').textContent = Pi ? f(vb) : 'no such thing';
    el('detOut').textContent = det(Pm).toFixed(3);
    el('aeOut').textContent = state.map === 'none' ? '—' : fm(Ae);
    el('abOut').textContent = state.map === 'none' ? '—' : fm(Ab);
    el('avOut').textContent = state.map === 'none' ? '—' : f(av);

    setEq([
      [
        ['\\mathbf v = P\\,[\\mathbf v]_B', WARM],
        '\\quad',
        ['[\\mathbf v]_B = P^{-1}\\mathbf v', TEAL],
        '\\quad',
        ['A = P A\' P^{-1}', HOT],
      ],
      Pi
        ? [
            [`[\\mathbf v]_B = (${vb.x.toFixed(2)},\\,${vb.y.toFixed(2)})`, TEAL],
            [`\\quad \\mathbf v = (${v.x.toFixed(2)},\\,${v.y.toFixed(2)})`, WARM],
            [`\\quad \\det P = ${det(Pm).toFixed(3)}`, ACC],
          ]
        : [['\\det P = 0,\\; \\text{no change of basis exists}', HOT]],
      [
        state.map === 'none'
          ? 'the arrow is one object; the numbers under it belong to whoever is holding the ruler'
          : 'read the sandwich right to left: translate, do the easy thing, translate back',
      ],
    ]);
  }

  // --- controls --------------------------------------------------------------

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
  const inp = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;

  const SLIDERS: [string, () => number, (n: number) => void][] = [
    ['b1x', () => b1.x, (n) => (b1.x = n)],
    ['b1y', () => b1.y, (n) => (b1.y = n)],
    ['b2x', () => b2.x, (n) => (b2.x = n)],
    ['b2y', () => b2.y, (n) => (b2.y = n)],
    ['vx', () => v.x, (n) => (v.x = n)],
    ['vy', () => v.y, (n) => (v.y = n)],
  ];
  function syncSliders() {
    for (const [id, get] of SLIDERS) {
      inp(id).value = String(get());
      el(id + 'val').textContent = get().toFixed(2);
    }
  }
  for (const [id, , set] of SLIDERS)
    inp(id).addEventListener('input', () => {
      set(+inp(id).value);
      el(id + 'val').textContent = (+inp(id).value).toFixed(2);
      draw();
    });

  el('presets').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#presets button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    const [x1, y1, x2, y2] = btn.dataset.b!.split(',').map(Number);
    b1.x = x1; b1.y = y1; b2.x = x2; b2.y = y2;
    syncSliders();
    draw();
  });

  inp('foreign').addEventListener('change', () => {
    state.foreign = inp('foreign').checked;
    draw();
  });

  el('map').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#map button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    state.map = btn.dataset.t!;
    draw();
  });

  // --- dragging, in whichever panel the pointer is over ----------------------

  type Handle = 'b1' | 'b2' | 'v';
  let drag: { h: Handle; p: Panel } | null = null;
  function local(e: PointerEvent) {
    const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    return {
      x: ((e.clientX - r.left) / r.width) * W,
      y: ((e.clientY - r.top) / r.height) * H,
    };
  }
  const target = (h: Handle) => (h === 'b1' ? b1 : h === 'b2' ? b2 : v);
  function hitTest(l: { x: number; y: number }) {
    for (const p of PANELS()) {
      if (l.x < p.x || l.x > p.x + p.w || l.y < p.y || l.y > p.y + p.h) continue;
      if (p.native === 'theirs' && !inv(P())) continue;
      for (const h of ['v', 'b1', 'b2'] as Handle[]) {
        const s = screen(p, target(h));
        if (Math.hypot(l.x - s.px, l.y - s.py) < 16) return { h, p };
      }
    }
    return null;
  }
  canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => {
    drag = hitTest(local(e));
    if (drag) canvas.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
  });
  canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
    const l = local(e);
    if (!drag) {
      canvas.style.cursor = hitTest(l) ? 'grab' : 'default';
      return;
    }
    // Undo the panel's own transform, so a drag in their grid moves the arrow
    // to where the pointer is in their coordinates, not ours.
    const c = centre(drag.p);
    const seen = { x: (l.x - c.x) / UNIT, y: (c.y - l.y) / UNIT };
    const back = inv(drag.p.T);
    if (!back) return;
    const w = app(back, seen);
    const snap = (n: number) => (Math.abs(n - Math.round(n)) < 0.08 ? Math.round(n) : +n.toFixed(2));
    const t = target(drag.h);
    const lim = drag.h === 'v' ? 5 : 3;
    t.x = Math.max(-lim, Math.min(lim, snap(w.x)));
    t.y = Math.max(-lim, Math.min(lim, snap(w.y)));
    syncSliders();
    draw();
  });
  const stop = () => (drag = null);
  canvas.addEventListener('pointerup', stop);
  canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', stop);

  syncSliders();
  draw();
</script>

